83
00:08:30,336 --> 00:08:30,976
cool.

84
00:08:32,334 --> 00:08:34,016
So, hello everyone, everyone.

85
00:08:34,096 --> 00:08:35,812
I'm Boris, as Marco said.

86
00:08:36,736 --> 00:08:44,356
So today's goal is to gain a high-level understanding of some of the more modern methods used in computer vision, which have then been applied to robotics.

87
00:08:45,296 --> 00:09:02,416
And so the readings or where I source this material is kind of from a few sources here and there, mainly from the courses here at Stanford CS231A and CS231N, as well as some various computer vision conference papers, which kind of just to illustrate a few examples.

88
00:09:02,496 --> 00:09:09,444
I mean, we're not expecting you to go and read the latest and greatest, although, you know, by all means that would be something very interesting as well.

89
00:09:11,182 --> 00:09:21,536
So to kick things off, let's discuss a little bit about where we are currently as a course in the perception stack, or at least in the computer vision timeline.

90
00:09:21,856 --> 00:09:36,516
So starting from the pinhole camera and kind of making our way up to you know, feature detectors, descriptors, and et cetera, we've covered you know, from time periods in the 1500s to kind of the this early 2000s period of computer vision research.

91
00:09:37,216 --> 00:09:48,176
And specifically what I'm interested in talking about today and giving you guys an insight on is what has happened from this early 2000s period to about 2012, and from 2012 and onward.

92
00:09:48,256 --> 00:09:52,708
And we're going to talk about why that's a special time in recent computer vision history.

93
00:09:53,902 --> 00:10:10,660
So to kick things off, just as a high-level overview, a lot of early 2000s computer vision was really dominated by you know, enforcing structure and inductive biases into the methods and approaches that were used in computer vision.

94
00:10:11,054 --> 00:10:13,616
And you know, feature engineering really reigns supreme.

95
00:10:13,696 --> 00:10:20,416
And you guys have gotten a taste of this by seeing stuff, you know, um topics such as edge detection, corner detection, blob detection.

96
00:10:20,496 --> 00:10:23,236
Okay, maybe you haven't seen that in class yet, or you you won't.

97
00:10:23,662 --> 00:10:33,444
Um, key point detectors and descriptors, you know, methods like sift, surf, orb, etc., and answering questions like how can you best describe a point in an image.

98
00:10:33,838 --> 00:10:46,532
And so these methods here, they are really, what they are is really applying human ingenuity and engineering skill into extracting as much information as possible out of raw pixels.

99
00:10:47,936 --> 00:11:04,644
And so now what we want to do is, you know, these are very interesting methods, but we want to shift our focus from one of focusing on image processing and how we can extract information from images to what problems can we solve with this information.

100
00:11:04,910 --> 00:11:10,948
And more specifically for this course, what are we even caring about with regards to computer vision for robotics?

101
00:11:11,536 --> 00:11:16,900
And so we want to shift our thinking from a method-specific focus to a problem-specific focus.

102
00:11:17,216 --> 00:11:26,884
And I'm going to describe today a few different important problems that you know come up in robotics and some approaches from computer vision that have been used to solve them.

103
00:11:28,366 --> 00:11:33,456
So, one of the first main classes of problems is object detection and classification.

104
00:11:33,856 --> 00:11:43,876
And you know, at a broad level, this is the problem of finding if there is an object of interest in an image and also classifying what that object is.

105
00:11:44,656 --> 00:12:03,652
And so the general solution idea is, or at least pre-2012, the general solution idea is to describe a region of an image, classify that region, and then shift your focus to some other or the next part of the image and rinse and repeat.

106
00:12:04,302 --> 00:12:12,708
And so this kind of iterative process is how you might be able to handle images of different sizes or different scales.

107
00:12:14,286 --> 00:12:18,532
And to dive a little bit deeper into some of the methods that have been employed in this space.

108
00:12:19,488 --> 00:12:29,888
so towards describing a region of an image, we've had methods like HOG, or histogram of oriented gradients, SIFT, which is a key point description.

109
00:12:29,888 --> 00:12:32,688
method, patch similarity, code words.

110
00:12:32,768 --> 00:12:34,596
these are some other methods that were used.

111
00:12:35,598 --> 00:12:45,248
Then in order to classify that description, usually some methods from applied probability and statistics or machine learning have been used, such as Knife Bayes, nearest neighbor SVMs, et cetera.

112
00:12:45,408 --> 00:12:45,728
Doesn't matter.

113
00:12:45,968 --> 00:12:51,492
You don't have to know these terms, just these are some of the examples of the different methodologies used for this specific sub-problem.

114
00:12:52,078 --> 00:12:57,008
And then to shift our focus to the next window, okay, there's not really too much, nothing special here.

115
00:12:57,088 --> 00:13:04,580
We've more just kind of looked at sliding window approaches where you might be looking at one part of the image, do some processing, and then look at the next part and keep going.

116
00:13:05,408 --> 00:13:21,968
And to give you guys an example of what this might look like pictorially, we're going to dive into one of the what I would say was most popular approaches along this scheme to solve object detection and classification.

117
00:13:22,048 --> 00:13:31,108
And that's specifically applying this idea of histogram oriented gradients with a classifier called a support vector machine.

118
00:13:31,406 --> 00:13:39,684
Again, it doesn't matter that you know these terms, it's just that this is kind of the exemplary method which really gives us an idea of what was going on at the time.

119
00:13:40,270 --> 00:13:41,988
So what would this method do?

120
00:13:42,368 --> 00:13:45,008
Well, you would start kind of as we discussed before.

121
00:13:45,168 --> 00:13:48,068
You have these three key steps that you're doing and then looping over.

122
00:13:48,494 --> 00:13:51,940
So the first is you pick some region in an image that you're interested in describing.

123
00:13:53,230 --> 00:14:01,444
You then extract some feature descriptor of that, and specifically, this is what something like histogram of oriented gradients might give you.

124
00:14:02,766 --> 00:14:09,060
it is, as the name implies, literally a histogram of the pixel gradients in that space.

125
00:14:10,350 --> 00:14:12,292
Gradients like edges, for example.

126
00:14:13,134 --> 00:14:18,468
And then from these features, because these really are just a set of numbers, so you can kind of make a really big vector out of this.

127
00:14:19,150 --> 00:14:23,248
And then what you might do from this is you might take this vector and then try and classify it.

128
00:14:23,502 --> 00:14:36,036
And when I say classify, I mean you might have a big set of vectors which are extracted from images of people's faces, and you might say, okay, all of these vectors are faces, and then all of these other vectors are not faces.

129
00:14:36,270 --> 00:14:49,028
And so that's what that's exactly actually what this image here is trying to show is that you know an SVM is a kind of linear classifier which will look at whatever vectors you have and draw a line between them in order to segment out two different classes.

130
00:14:50,190 --> 00:15:02,308
And so what you might do is, or what you would do is you would run these points through your classifier, obtain some score, whether that might be you know how likely it is to be a face or not.

131
00:15:02,990 --> 00:15:06,820
And then based on some threshold, you would consider this a detection or not.

132
00:15:07,246 --> 00:15:12,688
So in this case, you know, our score is, I don't know, 0.3 or something, which is not very indicative of a face.

133
00:15:12,768 --> 00:15:14,756
So we're not going to add this to our list of detections.

134
00:15:15,438 --> 00:15:17,700
All right, so what would we do next?

135
00:15:17,966 --> 00:15:28,036
Well, we slide our region of interest over, perform the exact same feature description, run it through our linear classifier, again, same lower score.

136
00:15:28,878 --> 00:15:38,084
And then you basically really just continue this up until maybe you actually get to some region or object of interest, run it through the detector.

137
00:15:38,608 --> 00:15:53,028
and now we might have some higher score because for the, you know, for the kinds of vectors, for the kinds of classification problem that this model was trained on, it is able to detect that this is a face, and it is able to give us a high score or a high confidence that this is a face.

138
00:15:53,678 --> 00:16:07,524
And so now when we have this, okay, what we're gonna do is we're gonna well add this coordinate as well as maybe the size of the filter to our, let's say, bounding box list, and then keep that in mind for when we're going to report our detection result after.

139
00:16:09,038 --> 00:16:15,504
So that's you know one general class of problems, and that was kind of one general solution method for that.

140
00:16:15,744 --> 00:16:22,468
Another important class of problems, especially in a robotics setting, is something called scene understanding.

141
00:16:22,894 --> 00:16:29,604
And scene understanding is well generally kind of understanding what's going on in the environment around you or the environment around a robot.

142
00:16:30,094 --> 00:16:34,532
And one interesting problem that we're going to be looking at today is something called semantic segmentation.

143
00:16:35,214 --> 00:16:43,396
And what that is, is the problem of developing an understanding of what's going on in this scene around you by classifying every observed point.

144
00:16:44,206 --> 00:16:48,384
And so here's an example of what a semantic segmentation might look like.

145
00:16:48,544 --> 00:16:59,556
So if this is like a camera observed image of some, I guess this is like a simulated environment, then here you can see all of the pixels are colored based on what class they belong to.

146
00:16:59,854 --> 00:17:06,660
And so, you might have classes like roads, vehicles, sidewalks, pedestrians, traffic signs, etc.

147
00:17:07,278 --> 00:17:10,304
And these are important for a robot to understand, because, well.

148
00:17:10,384 --> 00:17:13,424
obviously they also encode regions of importance in images.

149
00:17:13,504 --> 00:17:17,700
and you know, you definitely want to segment out something like a traffic light compared to something like a tree.

150
00:17:18,542 --> 00:17:20,420
and you know, where you might direct your focus to.

151
00:17:21,264 --> 00:17:30,944
And I would say pre-2016, there were these two general classes of methods for solving such a problem.

152
00:17:31,534 --> 00:17:35,044
One was clustering based, and then the other was graph-based.

153
00:17:35,310 --> 00:17:38,052
And we're going to actually look at some examples of both of these today.

154
00:17:38,864 --> 00:17:46,852
So clustering-based segmentation, and actually specifically within clustering-based, we're going to talk about k-means.

155
00:17:48,110 --> 00:18:04,516
And just before I dive any deeper, what k-means clustering is, is you know, at a very high level, you are it is a technique for segmenting unlabeled data into groups or into potentially semantically meaningful groups.

156
00:18:04,974 --> 00:18:08,784
And so this picture here really describes what the goal of clustering is.

157
00:18:08,864 --> 00:18:20,864
You know, if I give you just a spattering of points in who knows what high-dimensional space, the idea is can we recover maybe what groups they might belong to or maybe what individual classes they might actually encode?

158
00:18:21,134 --> 00:18:25,744
And so, for example, here at the bottom, well, this is, I guess, like the actual ground truth.

159
00:18:25,824 --> 00:18:27,652
This is like a this would be a perfect clustering.

160
00:18:27,918 --> 00:18:34,596
So this is kind of showing us that there's actually three separate distinct clusters that form this image here.

161
00:18:35,744 --> 00:18:44,036
And specifically, what k-means is for us is a method of grouping together like or similar features in their feature space.

162
00:18:44,704 --> 00:18:57,796
And the reason it's called k-means, and why there's that k there, is because inside the algorithm that you would run in order to obtain such a clustering, you actually have to choose how many clusters you want to segment out a priori.

163
00:18:57,998 --> 00:19:08,900
So it's a little bit of a, there's a little bit of a hyperparameter tuning game that you have to play, you know, how many clusters k is the best, and kind of check how your performance is with regards to different k's.

164
00:19:09,710 --> 00:19:12,704
Now I won't dive into the actual algorithm for this method.

165
00:19:12,878 --> 00:19:19,664
It's actually quite simple, and I really implore you to Google or search online what the algorithm looks like.

166
00:19:19,744 --> 00:19:26,916
There's some very nice animations of how the algorithm actually segments out points, but that's not the important part here today.

167
00:19:28,046 --> 00:19:36,804
The important part here today is to understand how we might use such a clustering method to actually segment out similar points in an image.

168
00:19:37,934 --> 00:19:44,804
And the idea, the key idea here is actually to map each pixel in an image into a five-dimensional feature space.

169
00:19:45,264 --> 00:19:48,740
Specifically X, Y, and then RGB.

170
00:19:50,254 --> 00:19:59,584
And then what you do is once these pixels are mapped to this feature space, you can then try and cluster them out using K-means or whatever.

171
00:20:00,304 --> 00:20:04,356
And why would we do this feature space?

172
00:20:04,624 --> 00:20:13,024
Well, the intuitive part of this is that in this five dimensions, x and y encode an element of spatial locality.

173
00:20:13,104 --> 00:20:24,516
And what this means is that you know, in an image, we intuitively believe that if one part of an image is close to another part of the image, then they're probably going to be related to the same object in 3D.

174
00:20:25,584 --> 00:20:29,584
I mean, unless you have some very sharp gradient there, which you could, maybe there's an edge or something like that.

175
00:20:29,870 --> 00:20:42,404
But if I were to take an image of the room right now, and I pick one pixel on that, one red chair there, and then look 10 pixels over, and it's the same red color, it's probably going to be the same chair, for example.

176
00:20:43,584 --> 00:20:53,604
And then why you have the RGB is that this is kind of encoding our intuition that similar things will have similar colors.

177
00:20:54,382 --> 00:20:59,300
And that's kind of just encoding like a like a sense of visual locality rather than just spatial locality.

178
00:21:00,878 --> 00:21:13,572
And so if we apply this method, so here we have just like a normal image, I don't know, let's say taken from a plane or a drone of some beach scene, and we maybe want to cluster out what the different parts of this image are.

179
00:21:13,998 --> 00:21:21,924
If we run this, you know, feature, we map each pixel to this five-dimensional feature space, and we run k means clustering on this feature space.

